WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.451 --> 00:00:03.900
Human babies are kind of pathetic.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.480
We can't run from
predators, feed ourselves,

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.960
or even lift up our own heads.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.000
But not all newborns are totally helpless.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.780
After all, it's a
treacherous world out there,

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.083
and some babies must go
to extremes to survive.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.890
Take barnacle geese.

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:24.730
They lay their eggs 120 meters up

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:27.320
the side of the cliff,
away from predators.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:31.300
That's the same height
as a 36-story building.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.100
But there's no food for
the chicks to eat up there.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:38.220
So when they're a few days
old, still incapable of flight,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.813
they leap off the cliff.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.690
Thousands are injured or killed on impact.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:47.290
But the lightest, fluffiest
chicks survive by floating down

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:49.533
and landing on their soft stomachs.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.520
Baby marine iguanas also have it rough.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.930
They don't have to leap off a cliff,

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:59.730
they just have to outrun
swarms of hungry snakes.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.070
Mothers lay their eggs
in underground burrows

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:03.210
for protection,

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:06.290
but they don't stick around
after laying the eggs.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:09.780
By the time the babies hatch
up to four months later,

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.670
the Galapagos racer snakes
have gathered for a feast.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.000
So the hatchlings have two options.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.430
Stay put and starve or make a run for it.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.610
The snakes are attracted to movement,

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.770
so the second the iguanas
take off, snakes give chase,

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.240
and only the quickest survive.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.200
While iguana hatchlings
must be fleet of foot,

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.700
greater guinea pigs are
speedy in a different way.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.550
Pups can have babies of their own,

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:39.810
just one month after birth.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.280
That's like humans giving
birth at 11 months old.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.130
And unlike other rodents,

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:48.670
the pups are born with
furry coats, open eyes,

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:51.460
and can walk on their first day.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:53.950
They're pretty much just mini-adults.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:55.590
Disturbing, right?

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.620
Just wait till you hear
about braconid wasps.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.960
Their babies are extreme predators

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.910
from the moment they hatch.

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.860
Adults hijack a caterpillar

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.760
and lay over 100 eggs underneath its skin.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:10.890
Once hatched, the larvae feed on

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:13.360
the caterpillar's internal organs,

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.890
and once it's on the brink of death,

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:19.472
the larvae chew their
way out, spin cocoons,

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:22.710
and emerge as fully formed adults.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.283
Ready to have deadly babies of their own.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:30.300
Speaking of which, sand tiger sharks eat

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.950
their own siblings
before they're even born.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.920
You see, mom has many suitors,

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.350
and they all fertilize her
eggs at different times.

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:43.980
So the eggs that are fertilized
early on develop sooner

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.440
in the womb before the rest,

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:47.980
and once they're big enough,

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:52.183
they eat their less developed siblings.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:56.864
And you thought you had annoying siblings.

